"""The time-series hand-off routes: the generated table AND the front-door directives that serve it.

`/go/ts/<survey>/<station>/<level>` 302s to the one NCI THREDDS fileServer URL for that file, and
404s for every path the table does not name. Two artifacts, pinned together here because neither is
safe alone:

  * `deploy/frontdoor/ts-routes.map` - GENERATED by deploy/scripts/gen_ts_routes.py from the
    per-survey verified-resource registers and COMMITTED, one line per projected (survey, station,
    level). It is the FIFTH leak artifact of the module's section-2 sweep and the only one that is not
    JSON, which is why its sweep lives here rather than in the engine's root-level sweep.
  * the Caddyfile map + two handles that turn it into a redirect, and refuse everything else.

THE TABLE IS THE SUPPRESSION (R5). `has_time_series` is projected for withheld stations too
(existence semantics), so mtcat.json hands an enumerator every segment of one of these paths:
suppression cannot live in discovery and must live in RESOLUTION. The map admits ONLY open-access,
`review: verified`, non-level2 rows, so an unlisted path cannot produce a Location at all - the
map's `default ""` 404s it by construction rather than by filter. A front-door leak is invisible to
an engine-side rglob, which is why this pin lives in deploy/tests.

EVERY NEGATIVE CARRIES A SENSITIVITY CONTROL: the same path, with the row admitted, resolves. A dead
matcher or a mis-built fixture would otherwise pass every 404 pin for the wrong reason.

The textual + generator legs run everywhere (stdlib + PyYAML). Where a caddy binary is on PATH the
directives are additionally driven against a REAL Caddy, exactly as test_frontdoor_pathurl_contract
does; CI installs caddy (gateway-ci.yml), so the runtime legs run there.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import json
import re
import shutil
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import urllib.error
import urllib.request
from pathlib import Path

import pytest

